course: dictionary
lesson: 8920
title: Sidewinding on hot sand
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sidewinder is a small rattlesnake from the deserts of the south-western USA and Mexico. The word can also mean any snake that moves the same way. Sidewinding is its special way of moving: the snake throws its body in loops across the sand, sideways rather than straight ahead.
explain: Use the context to work out the meaning. «The sidewinder snake moves sideways and touches the hot sand at only two points, leaving J-shaped tracks.» Moving sideways and leaving tracks in the sand tells you how it moves. Check by swapping your meaning in: "The sidewinder snake moves by throwing its body in loops" still makes sense.
explain: Think of hot sand on a beach that burns your feet. «A sidewinder snake lifts most of its body off the sand» so less of its body touches the heat. This helps it move on loose, hot sand, which is why scientists study how it moves.
fact: A sidewinder is a small rattlesnake from the deserts of the south-western USA and Mexico.
fact: Sidewinding means moving sideways by throwing the body in loops across the sand.
fact: Sidewinding lets a snake move on loose, hot sand with only small parts of its body touching the ground.
fact: Some vipers in African deserts also move this way, so sidewinder can mean any snake that sidewinds.
fact: Sidewinders have little horns above their eyes, and they leave J-shaped tracks in the sand.
exercise: «The sidewinder snake moves sideways and touches the hot sand at only two points.» What is a sidewinder here? | *A snake that moves sideways in loops across the sand | A fast bird that flies over the desert | A spider with long legs | A cold river in the mountains
exercise: In science, what does «sidewinding» mean? | Swimming fast in a deep river | *Moving sideways by throwing the body in loops | Climbing up tall trees | Sleeping in the shade all day
exercise: Where does a sidewinder rattlesnake live? | Deep oceans far from any land | Frozen lakes in the Arctic | *The deserts of the south-western USA and Mexico | Busy city streets in London
test: «Sidewinders leave J-shaped tracks in the sand.» What shape do their tracks make? | Triangle | Circle | Straight line | *J shape
test: Why does a sidewinder lift most of its body off the hot sand? | To sleep in the sun | *So less of its body touches the hot sand | To hide from the wind | To catch flying insects
exam: Jayden thinks a sidewinder is a bird. Using the word «sidewinder», what should he say? | Yes, it lives in the sea | No, it is a kind of cactus | *No, a sidewinder is a snake, a small rattlesnake from the desert | Yes, it flies over the sand
